# Line endings, decided here rather than per-developer.
#
# There was no .gitattributes, so every checkout's line endings depended
# on that machine's core.autocrlf. On Windows (autocrlf=true) a fresh
# clone gets CRLF; ruff format wants LF, so `ruff format --check` failed
# on 184 of 195 files in a clean clone while passing in a working tree
# where ruff itself had written the files. Same commit, same ruff,
# opposite answers -- which is exactly the local/CI disagreement this
# file exists to remove.
#
# The index has always stored LF. This makes the WORKING TREE match it
# for the file types that tooling cares about, so what a linter sees is
# what CI sees.

# Everything git considers text: normalise to LF in the index.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The types where a formatter or parser is opinionated about endings.
*.py    text eol=lf
*.pyi   text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.cfg   text eol=lf
*.ini   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts genuinely need CRLF.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Binary: never touched, never diffed as text.
*.parquet binary
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.zip     binary
*.har     binary
